00:23:44.000 --> 00:24:17.000
And it's really hard to know what we know about flow. And it is this very kind of powerful, compelling experience that, you know, we all have. And I think we seek sometimes. But what exactly it is and, you know, what it looks like in the brain and the body, how exactly it benefits us and when we really don't have a lot of good evidence on that. So, you know, I became interested in flow because of waiting and because of its fit as a coping strategy during waiting periods. but now I and my graduate students have fallen down the rabbit hole a little bit and are interested in getting a better handle on what that exactly is.
而且真的很难知道我们对心流了解什么。它是这种非常，你知道，强大、引人注目的体验，你知道，我们都有。而且我认为我们有时会寻求它。但它到底是什么，你知道，它在大脑和身体中看起来是什么样子，它到底如何使我们受益，以及何时我们真的没有很多好的证据。所以，你知道，我因为等待以及它作为等待期间应对策略的契合度而对心流产生了兴趣。但现在我和我的研究生们有点掉进了兔子洞，并且有兴趣更好地掌握它到底是什么。

45
00:24:17.000 --> 00:24:49.000
So that's one area. I'm also taking a deeper dive into worry and what exactly it is about worry during waiting periods that is so difficult. And that is work I'm doing with a collaborator at another University of California campus at Merced, Jennifer Howell, who's a longtime collaborator of mine. And we have some money right now to try to figure out, you know, when is it that worry is hopeful to us and not so disruptive to our lives? And when is it that really does become a problem for our health and well-being? And that turns out to be, as things
